<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  LiftPath themes.

  Structure:

    Theme.LiftPath.Base           everything palette-INDEPENDENT: type, shape, dialogs,
                                  system bars. Binds no lp* tokens.
    Theme.Fitness                 the manifest default = Base + just enough colour for the
                                  system's starting window. Named Theme.Fitness so the
                                  manifest and older references need no change.
    ThemeOverlay.LiftPath.Paper   the palette bindings — 27 lp* tokens plus the Material
                       .Chalk     colour roles. Applied at runtime by LiftPathApplication
                       .Bone      in onActivityPreCreated, i.e. before any inflation.
                       .Steel
                       .Cloud
                       .Ash
                       .Sand
                       .Fog

  Why OVERLAYS rather than eight full themes: `Activity.setTheme` merges rather than
  replaces, so applying a full app theme over an activity that declares its own
  (RestTimerDialogActivity uses Theme.AppCompat.Dialog so it can float over the lock
  screen) would override windowIsFloating/windowBackground and render it as an opaque
  full-screen activity. An overlay carries only colour, so every activity gets the tokens
  regardless of its base theme, and nothing needs exempting.

  There are deliberately NO values-night variants here. Day/night is handled one level
  down: colours come from values-night/lp_palette_*.xml, the two system-bar booleans from
  values-night/lp_bools.xml. Eight overlays, not sixteen.

  Which overlay actually applies is resolved by AppearanceManager.resolvedTheme(), not
  fixed per palette any more: the user picks a palette for light mode and a (possibly
  different) one for dark mode, and whichever is active right now supplies the overlay.
  A palette's own values-night file is still what makes IT look right once chosen for
  dark — this file only decides which palette, not light vs. dark within it.

  Adding a ninth palette = one lp_palette_*.xml pair, one overlay here, one enum entry in
  AppearanceManager. No layout should ever need touching.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- ==================================================================
         BASE — palette-independent
         ================================================================== -->
    <style name="Theme.LiftPath.Base" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar">

        <!-- Type. Catches widgets that resolve their face from the theme rather than an
             explicit textAppearance (dialogs, tabs, menus). -->
        <item name="android:fontFamily">@font/archivo</item>
        <item name="fontFamily">@font/archivo</item>

        <!-- Shape -->
        <item name="shapeAppearanceSmallComponent">@style/ShapeAppearance.LP.Small</item>
        <item name="shapeAppearanceMediumComponent">@style/ShapeAppearance.LP.Medium</item>
        <item name="shapeAppearanceLargeComponent">@style/ShapeAppearance.LP.Large</item>

        <!-- Components. materialCardViewStyle is deliberately NOT set globally: several
             screens still mix MaterialCardView with legacy CardView, and flattening only
             the Material half would look broken rather than better. Layouts opt in with
             style="@style/Widget.LP.Card" as each migration wave lands. -->
        <item name="tabStyle">@style/Widget.LP.TabLayout</item>

        <!-- Edge-to-edge system bars. Icon-contrast flags come from a night-aware bool so
             this block stays palette- and mode-independent. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">@bool/lp_light_system_bars</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="o_mr1">@bool/lp_light_system_bars</item>

        <!-- Dialogs -->
        <item name="materialAlertDialogTheme">@style/ThemeOverlay.Fitness.MaterialAlertDialog</item>
        <item name="bottomSheetDialogTheme">@style/ThemeOverlay.Fitness.BottomSheetDialog</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      Manifest default.

      Carries only windowBackground and colorSurface, because the *system* resolves this
      theme to draw the starting window before the process exists — that happens too early
      for any overlay to have been applied, and an unresolvable windowBackground is a build
      error. Everything else is left to the overlay, which lands in onActivityPreCreated
      before inflation, so nothing ever actually renders with unbound tokens.

      Consequence: a user on Steel sees a Paper-coloured starting window for one frame. All
      four canvases are near-white in light mode and near-black in dark, so it is
      imperceptible in practice.

      The windowSplashScreen* items below are drawn by the OS before this process exists,
      so — same reasoning — they cannot reflect the chosen palette either. They are a
      neutral, self-contained badge (fixed dark background behind a flat white mark) that
      reads fine regardless of which palette lands a moment later. The actual themed
      reveal happens in MainActivity's splashScreen.setOnExitAnimationListener, once
      AppearanceManager is readable.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Fitness" parent="Theme.LiftPath.Base">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/paper_canvas</item>
        <item name="colorSurface">@color/paper_surface</item>

        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/paper_canvas</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_app_logo</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@color/paper_ink</item>
    </style>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         PALETTE OVERLAYS
         Each binds the lp* roles AND the Material colour roles. The Material half
         matters: ~14 layouts use Widget.MaterialComponents.Button / .Chip.* /
         .TextInputLayout.* explicitly, and those resolve their fills from
         colorPrimary / colorSurface / colorOnSurface — so setting these is what themes
         every Material component without touching a layout.
         ================================================================== -->

